VERDICT
Correct: your code brilliantly handles the 1-indexed base-26 system by subtracting 1 before each step and builds the string efficiently.

REVIEW MY CODE

What I did well: You correctly realized that Excel columns don't have a "zero", so you subtracted 1 (temp--) to fix the math. You also built the string using push_back and reversed it at the end, which is much faster than inserting characters at the front of the string one by one.

Is it correct? Yes, it is fully correct and passes all test cases.

Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses: There are no logical bugs. However, using the magic number 65 is not good practice. You should use 'A' instead, so anyone reading your code knows exactly what you mean without memorising ASCII tables. Also, you don't really need the temp variable; you can just modify columnNumber directly.

Time and space complexity of MY code, each with a one-line reason: Time complexity is O(log n) because you divide the number by 26 at each step, shrinking it very fast. Space complexity is O(1) auxiliary space because you only use a few variables to build the final answer.

Why my approach works, or why it doesn't: This is just like converting a standard decimal number into binary (base-2), except we are converting it into base-26. Because there is no "0" in Excel columns (A is 1, not 0), subtracting 1 perfectly shifts the number down so that standard math (modulo 26) works flawlessly.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED

Approach 1: String Prepending (The Slow Way)

Where it comes from: Common beginner mistake when trying to build the string left-to-right.

Idea: Do the exact same math, but add the new character to the front of the answer string immediately so you don't have to reverse it at the end.

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    string convertToTitle(int columnNumber) {
        string ans = "";
        while (columnNumber > 0) {
            columnNumber--;
            char c = 'A' + (columnNumber % 26);
            ans = c + ans;
            columnNumber /= 26;
        }
        return ans;
    }
};
Time and space complexity: Time is O((log n)^2) because adding a character to the front of a string forces the computer to shift all existing characters over by one spot every single time. Space is O(1) auxiliary space.

What makes the next approach better: Adding to the end of a string is instant, while adding to the front is slow. It's much faster to add to the end and reverse it once.

Approach 2: Modulo and Reverse (Your Approach - Optimal)

Where it comes from: Standard algorithm for base conversions taught in computer science.

Idea: Subtract 1 from the number to make it 0-indexed. Find the remainder when divided by 26 to get the letter, stick it on the end of our answer, and divide the number by 26 to move to the next place value. Finally, reverse the answer.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    string convertToTitle(int columnNumber) {
        string ans = "";
        while (columnNumber > 0) {
            columnNumber--;
            ans.push_back('A' + (columnNumber % 26));
            columnNumber /= 26;
        }
        reverse(ans.begin(), ans.end());
        return ans;
    }
};
Time and space complexity: Time is O(log n) base 26, which is extremely fast. Space is O(1) extra space.

What makes the next approach better: Approach 2 is already the most optimal way to solve this iteratively. We can only change the style, like using recursion for elegance.

Approach 3: Recursive Approach

Where it comes from: Functional programming enthusiasts and LeetCode discussion forums.

Idea: Instead of a while loop, we ask a function to find the letters for the remaining division, and then append the current letter at the very end. The function stack naturally builds the string in the correct order without needing a reverse function.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <string>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    string convertToTitle(int columnNumber) {
        if (columnNumber == 0) {
            return "";
        }
        columnNumber--;
        return convertToTitle(columnNumber / 26) + (char)('A' + (columnNumber % 26));
    }
};
Time and space complexity: Time is O(log n). Space is O(log n) because each recursive call takes up memory on the call stack.

WHERE I STAND

Which approach did I use? You used Approach 2, the Modulo and Reverse method, which is the most optimal.

Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem? They expect exactly your approach (Approach 2). They want to see that you remember to subtract 1 to handle the 1-based indexing, and they want to see you use string reverse instead of slow string prepending.

Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:
Question: What is the maximum length the output string can possibly be?
Answer: Since the maximum 32-bit signed integer is about 2.14 billion, and 26 to the power of 7 is greater than that, the string will never be longer than 7 characters.

WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER (for my revision notes)

The key insight, in one sentence: Excel columns act exactly like a Base-26 number system, but because there is no zero, you must subtract 1 from the number before each modulo and division step.

My mistakes, as a short checklist for next time: Avoid magic numbers like 65; use characters like 'A' so your code explains itself.

